WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.124
Container networking determines which services, networks, and endpoints workloads can communicate with.

2
00:00:07.124 --> 00:00:14.945
Exposed ports, service discovery, ingress, egress, API gateways, and load balancers all shape the reachable surface.

3
00:00:14.945 --> 00:00:20.366
A secure design does not assume every container should talk to every other service.

4
00:00:20.366 --> 00:00:27.955
Network policies, at a high level, help express allowed communication paths and reduce unnecessary lateral reach.

5
00:00:27.955 --> 00:00:30.897
Ingress and egress deserve equal attention.

6
00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:36.008
Ingress controls who can reach a service from outside or from other workloads.

7
00:00:36.008 --> 00:00:39.338
Egress controls where a workload can send traffic.

8
00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:46.695
Egress is often overlooked, but it matters for data protection, dependency control, and incident containment.

9
00:00:46.695 --> 00:00:53.045
A compromised or malfunctioning workload should not have unlimited network freedom by default.

10
00:00:53.045 --> 00:01:01.485
Orchestrator access control is critical because orchestrators can deploy, modify, inspect, connect to, and remove workloads.

11
00:01:01.485 --> 00:01:09.926
Service accounts, role-based access concepts, namespace or project boundaries, and administrative access review all matter.

12
00:01:09.926 --> 00:01:20.457
A user or workload with broad orchestrator permissions may be able to read secrets, change images, expose services, or observe sensitive application behavior.

13
00:01:20.457 --> 00:01:27.736
Admission policy concepts add another layer of control by checking deployment requests before workloads run.

14
00:01:27.736 --> 00:01:36.409
Policies may require trusted registries, non-root users, resource limits, approved image signatures, or restricted host mounts.

15
00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:46.399
The broader principle is governance: control who can deploy, what can be deployed, how it can communicate, and who can observe or modify it after release.
